  :from ~/.claude/history.jsonl#2026-01-02T21:17:09-07:00
  :project libudon

  |title My daughter is 7 and tells me her theories with a great deal of confidence

  |quote
    When I was a child, I would often come up with plausible (to me) rationale for various things I saw or experienced around me. My daughter who is 7 does the same thing-- often telling me her theories with a great deal of confidence. It's not a bad thing per se-- it is hypothesis generation-- it is the seed of understanding. But it isn't truth.

  |occasion
    In the middle of parser work, on how confidently-delivered reconstruction should be
    treated.

  |about
    The cleanest statement in the corpus of the plausibility-versus-verification
    distinction, and it is generous rather than scolding: confident theorising is *the
    seed of understanding* and is not to be suppressed. What is refused is only the
    promotion — hypothesis is not truth, and confidence is not the thing that converts
    it. He includes himself as the first example.

  |before-acting
    - You are about to assert something you inferred from shape rather than checked.
    - A structural argument feels inevitable and the next word forming is "therefore".
    - You are stating a confident reconstruction of something you have not opened.
    - You are explaining why something must work the way you think it does.

  |see
    - #exm-assume-you-are-wrong
    - #exm-high-signal-original-data

